1
00:00:06,473 --> 00:00:15,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha mentions the word samādhi, which is generally translated as concentration, or something like that.

2
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:26,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you think that's a mostly adequate word to translate it, or do you think there's something more appropriate that kind of describes the Pali word samādhi?

3
00:00:28,368 --> 00:00:34,793
[SPEAKER_01]: The... well, there's nothing wrong with a term concentration.

4
00:00:35,872 --> 00:00:36,452
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself.

5
00:00:36,833 --> 00:01:01,306
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem today is, due to many popularized meditation techniques and the contemporary take on the whole meditation practice, usually concentration ends up misrepresenting what was meant by samādhi and certainly prevents people from correctly doing it very often.

6
00:01:02,346 --> 00:01:03,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the better word

7
00:01:05,918 --> 00:01:08,039
[SPEAKER_01]: For samādhi would be a composure.

8
00:01:09,679 --> 00:01:18,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the whole notion of samādhi, samādhi in itself means keeping things together, binding together.

9
00:01:18,762 --> 00:01:29,166
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, you can say concentration means you bring something together, but it has much more narrower connotations than if you would say composure.

10
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:33,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that you have to compose and then maintain.

11
00:01:34,257 --> 00:01:43,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintain that composition that that takes takes different type of effort than the effort of focusing and effort of concentration.

12
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:50,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and also, concentration can can kind of indicate for people you concentrate on something, so you switch everything else out.

13
00:01:50,861 --> 00:01:56,202
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's more like you compose yourself above everything else altogether.

14
00:01:56,282 --> 00:01:58,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So that nothing can bother you in that sense.

15
00:01:58,163 --> 00:02:02,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you shut it all out, but because you you have gone beyond it.

16
00:02:04,257 --> 00:02:13,807
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people usually, from a commentarial point of view, talk about samādhi, it generally brings up this idea of a meditation object.

17
00:02:14,664 --> 00:02:32,173
[SPEAKER_00]: The Visuddhimagga, for example, lists 40 so-called objects ranging from casinos to various recollections of the triple gem and so on and so forth, that one becomes absorbed in and focuses on as much as possible.

18
00:02:32,873 --> 00:02:40,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, the common technique is that you lose your focus and then you bring yourself back to the meditation object.

19
00:02:41,192 --> 00:02:47,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any validity in that practice, or are you saying that it's kind of fundamentally misguided?

20
00:02:47,996 --> 00:02:52,839
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a completely wrong direction to go by, if you want to develop your mind.

21
00:02:53,579 --> 00:03:06,287
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to practice concentration, in a sense of concentration, that's fine, but the type of composure that the Buddha was encouraging and talking about is the one that's founded upon mindfulness.

22
00:03:07,358 --> 00:03:23,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, if people do concentration practice, samādhi practice, in terms of focusing, it requires you to focus on the particular, as in the object of your meditation, and you keep trying to observe it as closely as possible.

23
00:03:23,610 --> 00:03:34,057
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will inevitably, has to, that inevitably has to result in in losing the sight of the general awareness in regard to what you were doing, which is focusing on a particular object.

24
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:38,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what I'm saying is that if you are chasing the object, you cannot be mindful of doing that.

25
00:03:39,897 --> 00:03:55,008
[SPEAKER_01]: While if you start looking at the nature of object, the background of the arisen particular experience, you are actually playing along the basis of mindfulness that the Buddha did say you can't have too much of, and it's a good thing to develop.

26
00:03:58,010 --> 00:04:01,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it seems almost like the... almost an opposite of absorption.

27
00:04:03,581 --> 00:04:08,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, again, you can say absorption, but it's not absorption in one point that shuts everything else out.

28
00:04:09,325 --> 00:04:12,828
[SPEAKER_01]: It's absorption in the more general point that includes everything else in.

29
00:04:13,689 --> 00:04:25,118
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha gave such similes like a gatekeeper or a man who is too old to... who needs to look after the children, but he's too old to chase them individually.

30
00:04:25,198 --> 00:04:28,501
[SPEAKER_01]: If he chases one child, he loses sight of all the other children in the yard.

31
00:04:29,436 --> 00:04:35,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you do, you find a place from which you can see them all at the same time.

32
00:04:35,418 --> 00:04:39,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to the same extent, but at the same time.

33
00:04:41,561 --> 00:04:43,542
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense that a gatekeeper would know.

34
00:04:43,642 --> 00:04:48,904
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't chase people who come through the gate into the fenced-off area.

35
00:04:49,884 --> 00:04:50,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he knows.

36
00:04:50,585 --> 00:04:51,405
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to chase them.

37
00:04:51,545 --> 00:04:53,746
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that they're inside and that's all I need to know.

38
00:04:55,244 --> 00:05:06,189
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why any practice of mindfulness that results in the correct practice of composure, the Buddha would say, he knows there is a pleasant feeling present... pleasant feeling... pleasant feeling present as pleasant.

39
00:05:06,449 --> 00:05:08,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, oh, there's a pleasant feeling.

40
00:05:08,470 --> 00:05:10,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, oh, there is an unpleasant feeling.

41
00:05:10,931 --> 00:05:11,832
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a neutral feeling.

42
00:05:12,392 --> 00:05:13,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, there is a depressed mind present.

43
00:05:14,233 --> 00:05:15,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, there is a latent mind present.

44
00:05:16,534 --> 00:05:23,137
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that results in complete fulfillment of the satipaṭṭhāna, which results in fulfillment of samādhi as well.

45
00:05:23,898 --> 00:05:24,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

46
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:40,740
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite the opposite, then, if you were to say, oh, there is an elated mind present in regard to this experience, or that experience, or why is it elated, because of this is elated, and what did that result in, and what did it bring to be, and so on.

47
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:46,422
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually, you stop being concerned with a particular content, and you just look at the general nature of it.

48
00:05:47,263 --> 00:05:48,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the beginning of samādhi.

49
00:05:49,484 --> 00:05:55,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you're sitting cross-legged, whether you're doing walking meditation, whether you're standing or laying down,

50
00:05:56,762 --> 00:06:02,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness and samādhi, basically, are inseparable, if done rightly.

51
00:06:02,485 --> 00:06:07,687
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's certainly mindfulness... Unwavering mindfulness is what your samādhi becomes.

52
00:06:09,167 --> 00:06:15,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you must focus on something else, that will then result in your samādhi, and then you go back to practice your mindfulness.

53
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:18,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness, when developed, that's what samādhi is.

54
00:06:18,892 --> 00:06:24,454
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, with that kind of general knowledge of the nature thing, of the nature that is present, in regard to any particular thing,

55
00:06:24,922 --> 00:06:26,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly how you develop vipassana.

56
00:06:27,123 --> 00:06:29,965
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why samatha and vipassana go hand in hand.

57
00:06:30,005 --> 00:06:32,587
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot possibly separate them.

58
00:06:32,627 --> 00:06:43,794
[SPEAKER_01]: By having the correct samatha, correct ins... correct calmness in regard to that composure, means the clarity of the nature of things is present, because you're not absorbed, you're not pulled by the content.

59
00:06:45,635 --> 00:06:48,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So you... so your insight in regard to that nature is being developed.

60
00:06:49,863 --> 00:06:50,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that samatha.

61
00:06:51,223 --> 00:07:01,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And, and equally, if you, if you focus on the insight of the nature of the things, well, in order, well, knowing and discerning that nature means you're composing the right mindfulness right there and then.

62
00:07:02,825 --> 00:07:08,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, then samādhi and your samatha gets developed through your vipassana, in the correct sense of that word.

63
00:07:10,647 --> 00:07:15,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Typically, there's also this idea given of, like,

64
00:07:16,492 --> 00:07:20,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So-called stages or steps of samādhi.

65
00:07:20,935 --> 00:07:30,181
[SPEAKER_00]: The commentaries mention things like access concentration preceding jhāna, momentary concentration, things like that.

66
00:07:30,201 --> 00:07:44,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I mean, we don't have to go into those specifically unless you want to, but in terms of, I guess, is there a way to kind of quantify, like, more or less samādhi, or is that kind of misguided to start measuring to begin with?

67
00:07:45,723 --> 00:07:55,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can't measure it, because it would require you to step outside of yourself and somehow magically measure the extent of your concentration, which is, well, inconceivable.

68
00:07:56,068 --> 00:07:58,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, secondly, you never find any of that stuff in the suttas.

69
00:08:00,710 --> 00:08:14,856
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha did talk about meditation, he was always in... well, he was in very different terms than the terms that came later with the commentarial in a kind of contemporary Buddhist technique meditation, where you're catching the moments, observing them.

70
00:08:15,676 --> 00:08:23,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to... trying to, sort of, eh... get the right pure perception that will, sort of, tell you the truth behind the appearances and so on.

71
00:08:24,242 --> 00:08:34,069
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that, I think, we covered that in other talks, but all of that, kind of, assumes you, eh... well, not being aware of yourself to begin with, which means you're not mindful of the general background.

72
00:08:34,589 --> 00:08:41,414
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, secondary, also, eh... it is all very often founded upon the external view of yourself.

73
00:08:42,767 --> 00:08:51,870
[SPEAKER_01]: As if you can observe the pure moment, for example, that's independent of your experience as a whole, that would require you to somehow step outside of it, and that's just inconceivable.

74
00:08:52,210 --> 00:09:00,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It's conceivable only in the in the in the form of wrong conception, which is why it's a wrong view.

75
00:09:00,233 --> 00:09:12,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that leads to also, obviously, the Buddha distinguishes, we could say, between a wrong samādhi and a right samādhi, so far as there is a wrong eightfold path and a right eightfold path.

76
00:09:13,115 --> 00:09:18,519
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is it specifically that makes one's samādhi right, as opposed to not?

77
00:09:18,639 --> 00:09:20,421
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that direction that I'm talking about.

78
00:09:21,001 --> 00:09:28,647
[SPEAKER_01]: If your samādhi is concerned with composing the nature of things to be present and endured in your experience, that's the right samādhi, or samādhi along the right lines.

79
00:09:29,347 --> 00:09:35,152
[SPEAKER_01]: If your composure, concentration, is concerned around focusing on the particular, or whatever it's in front of you, and then within

80
00:09:35,852 --> 00:09:46,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Further particular, within that particular, within that particular, you're basically like preying upon something, you're hunting down the moment, chasing it, catching it, trying to see it in its arising, and that's a completely wrong direction.

81
00:09:47,577 --> 00:10:00,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha did say, gave that comparison, like, when a person doesn't know how to meditate rightly, he mis-meditates, pre-meditates, under-meditates, over-meditates, so to speak.

82
00:10:00,423 --> 00:10:01,344
[SPEAKER_01]: He cannot meditate.

83
00:10:01,904 --> 00:10:03,084
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he says it's like an owl

84
00:10:05,047 --> 00:10:12,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Praying upon, waiting for a mouse to show its head, so it can grab it, so it can catch it, so it can witness it, whatever you want to call it.

85
00:10:12,471 --> 00:10:13,771
[SPEAKER_01]: But the attitude is the same.

86
00:10:13,931 --> 00:10:16,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like a cat catching a fish or something like that.

87
00:10:16,593 --> 00:10:18,333
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ends up...

88
00:10:18,373 --> 00:10:28,058
[SPEAKER_01]: If your meditation ends up being about trying to perceive things in that sort of hunting manner,

89
00:10:28,577 --> 00:10:30,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you're trying to catch it, you're trying...

90
00:10:30,218 --> 00:10:33,559
[SPEAKER_01]: I must not miss, I must not blink, because the moment will slip me.

91
00:10:34,099 --> 00:10:37,240
[SPEAKER_01]: I must catch every moment as it arises.

92
00:10:37,260 --> 00:10:42,322
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha did compare that, same thing, to actually an animal hunting its prey.

93
00:10:43,503 --> 00:10:48,665
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, he said, that person who meditates like that, he meditates with centrality.

94
00:10:49,185 --> 00:10:49,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

95
00:10:49,385 --> 00:10:51,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's fully based upon the domain of the senses.

96
00:10:52,546 --> 00:10:54,167
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why that samādhi is wrong.

97
00:10:55,507 --> 00:10:56,868
[SPEAKER_01]: As I was explaining in another talk,

98
00:10:58,297 --> 00:11:02,440
[SPEAKER_01]: People often, sort of, will disagree with it, because, well, I find it helpful.

99
00:11:03,181 --> 00:11:10,546
[SPEAKER_01]: But, first of all, unless a person is already Sotāpanna, you don't know what true helpfulness is.

100
00:11:12,308 --> 00:11:21,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nobody's denying the relative benefit, but I'm highlighting the fact that it can only be a relative benefit, in a sense of it's a circumstantial benefit.

101
00:11:22,135 --> 00:11:27,199
[SPEAKER_01]: The focusing practice can only have a circumstantial benefit to one's peace of mind.

102
00:11:28,352 --> 00:11:39,179
[SPEAKER_01]: If one is looking for a long-term solution, needs to go the other direction, direction that includes the nature of the arisen phenomena, the nature of the arisen experience, which is where the nature of unwholesome is.

103
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:52,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So, becoming skilled in seeing things in that general manner, seeing the background behind the particular appearance, that is simultaneously present with it, that's how you develop the understanding of the nature of whatever's present in front of you.

104
00:11:53,649 --> 00:11:56,051
[SPEAKER_00]: So, related to that, here is a...

105
00:11:57,156 --> 00:11:59,617
[SPEAKER_00]: A sutta from the Anguttara Nikaya.

106
00:12:00,537 --> 00:12:03,058
[SPEAKER_00]: It's called the Saddha Sutta.

107
00:12:05,079 --> 00:12:09,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha is giving these instructions to a venerable Saddha.

108
00:12:10,200 --> 00:12:15,242
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, meditate like a thoroughbred horse, not like an inferior horse.

109
00:12:16,142 --> 00:12:18,503
[SPEAKER_00]: How does one meditate like an inferior horse?

110
00:12:19,443 --> 00:12:24,925
[SPEAKER_00]: When an inferior horse is tied up to the feeding trowel, he meditates, hey, hey.

111
00:12:25,561 --> 00:12:27,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Like h-a-y-a.

112
00:12:28,203 --> 00:12:28,924
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

113
00:12:29,304 --> 00:12:37,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when an inferior horse is tied up to the feeding trowel, he does not ask himself, what will the horse trainer make me do today?

114
00:12:37,591 --> 00:12:39,392
[SPEAKER_00]: How will I respond?

115
00:12:39,452 --> 00:12:42,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Tied up to the feeding trowel, he meditates, hey, hey.

116
00:12:43,075 --> 00:12:49,681
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way, sadhā, a certain inferior man, having gone to the forest, the root of a tree, an empty dwelling,

117
00:12:50,317 --> 00:12:53,539
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way, sadhā, one is a man who meditates like an inferior horse.

118
00:13:18,539 --> 00:13:20,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's exactly, exactly what I've been saying now.

119
00:13:21,041 --> 00:13:21,802
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like...

120
00:13:22,482 --> 00:13:28,387
[SPEAKER_01]: In the case of a horse who is not an inferior horse, he wouldn't just look at what's right in front of him, which is hay, hay.

121
00:13:29,027 --> 00:13:31,149
[SPEAKER_01]: He would think, what's implied in this?

122
00:13:32,149 --> 00:13:35,612
[SPEAKER_01]: What will the horse trainer make me do today?

123
00:13:35,732 --> 00:13:39,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he brought this hay for me, but that means something.

124
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:43,501
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the background of this presently arisen experience for me?

125
00:13:43,941 --> 00:13:45,982
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not just concentrating on what he wants.

126
00:13:46,422 --> 00:14:00,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And often, that's why often people approach, they have like a, they have a wholesome motivation, they have a right motivation, but they end up approaching meditation just to pure sensuality, just to trying, trying to get rid of the unpleasant

127
00:14:01,259 --> 00:14:14,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling or the or the possibility of unpleasant feeling arising, just through sheer sort of replacement with a good feeling, that I can keep on, I can actually just maintain it forever, and I'll never experience any lack of peace or discomfort.

128
00:14:15,627 --> 00:14:26,573
[SPEAKER_01]: But the true peace and freedom from discomfort comes from understanding the nature of the nature of feelings, all the feelings, not just replacing one with the other.

129
00:14:27,041 --> 00:14:36,605
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what people would go through, like often, through different ājāns and different techniques and different retreats, just trying to rekindle different novelty, different sort of scenarios.

130
00:14:37,245 --> 00:14:42,707
[SPEAKER_01]: They will, they will be able to replace their unpleasant existential feelings.

131
00:14:43,427 --> 00:14:50,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Or they have to keep reintensifying their, their, their focusing practices and so on, which then obviously results in some other drawbacks.

132
00:14:52,233 --> 00:14:55,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so, as that sutta says, you actually reflect.

133
00:14:55,976 --> 00:15:05,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in samādhi, the whole point of it is to see a bigger picture, not to be absorbed and concentrated upon right, like, right what's in front of your nose.

134
00:15:07,224 --> 00:15:11,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, generally, when the Buddha talks about samādhi, it always seems to be in the context of a

135
00:15:12,488 --> 00:15:14,269
[SPEAKER_00]: A path, like a gradual path.

136
00:15:14,329 --> 00:15:38,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, obviously, there's a Noble Eightfold Path, and there's a number of suttas, such as Majjhima Nikāya 39, the Greater Discourse at Asapura, that gives, like, a gradual training that starts with things like purifying one's conduct and leads one all the way to, you know, final nibbāna, leading through the hindrances and jhānas.

137
00:15:38,983 --> 00:15:41,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I won't read the whole sutta,

138
00:15:41,905 --> 00:15:44,087
[SPEAKER_00]: But just generally give the outline.

139
00:15:44,547 --> 00:16:05,163
[SPEAKER_00]: It starts with telling one to develop shame and fear of wrongdoing, to restrain one's bodily conduct, restrain one's verbal conduct, restrain one's mental conduct, practice restraint of the senses, moderation of eating, wakefulness, mindfulness and full awareness.

140
00:16:05,703 --> 00:16:10,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, all of those things, so I can just interject, is what's prepping you

141
00:16:11,895 --> 00:16:16,196
[SPEAKER_01]: for becoming more accustomed to dealing and seeing the background.

142
00:16:17,477 --> 00:16:25,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Because a non-restrained person is the person who goes with a with a particular content of their experience, which is a definition of sensuality.

143
00:16:26,279 --> 00:16:36,082
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, even if you become restrained beforehand, your views are still pertaining to the sensual domain, which is how people often end up expecting to perceive

144
00:16:36,827 --> 00:16:39,367
[SPEAKER_01]: The right moment and the moment of meditation and so on.

145
00:16:40,148 --> 00:16:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why all of this is a necessary prerequisite, because just by engaging in restraint, mindfulness, wakefulness, verbal restraint, mental restraint as well, you are actually stepping back into that more general picture and refusing to be pulled and fully absorbed by the content.

146
00:17:00,451 --> 00:17:02,992
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, samādhi should just be the extension of that.

147
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:08,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's a prerequisite for samādhi, not for something else.

148
00:17:08,642 --> 00:17:18,327
[SPEAKER_01]: But what most people end up doing is being very virtuous, very restrained, but when it comes to samādhi, they go back into same type of focusing, just a different object.

149
00:17:20,748 --> 00:17:24,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But the attitude of you staring at the object stays the same.

150
00:17:24,646 --> 00:17:35,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if that object tries to be a lofty general background or some some some generic idea or like a earth element or casino, you're doing it in a central manner through trying to perceive it and focus on it.

151
00:17:36,075 --> 00:17:37,196
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not stepping into it.

152
00:17:37,596 --> 00:17:41,079
[SPEAKER_01]: You're actually trying to absorb yourself into what you think it is.

153
00:17:43,260 --> 00:17:45,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's also inseparable from the view.

154
00:17:46,095 --> 00:17:49,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think it's a wrong thing, means your meditation will be wrong.

155
00:17:49,237 --> 00:17:55,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, if you think it's a thing that actually it isn't, your meditation will be wrong, because you think it wrongly.

156
00:17:55,800 --> 00:18:00,243
[SPEAKER_01]: You think this is what samādhi is, this is what composure is, but it isn't.

157
00:18:01,103 --> 00:18:04,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, all of your efforts will be equally wrong.

158
00:18:06,366 --> 00:18:10,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it sounds like if you understand what it means to be

159
00:18:15,811 --> 00:18:22,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, understanding what it means to be restrained, understanding the place of restraint, it's already the beginning of samādhi, the beginning of composure.

160
00:18:23,115 --> 00:18:23,896
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha was saying.

161
00:18:23,976 --> 00:18:30,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the skilled monk would establish his mind, and then he said, like even in the first jhāna,

162
00:18:31,155 --> 00:18:42,439
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, whether he walks, sits down, eats, urinates or defecates, whatever he does, it's divine, because his mind is in such a lofty established state.

163
00:18:43,319 --> 00:18:47,260
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do that if you're just focused on one thing and you forget about yourself entirely.

164
00:18:48,381 --> 00:18:52,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, you can get so established to the point of cessation of perception and feeling.

165
00:18:53,082 --> 00:19:00,165
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's the ultimate point that includes everything else that can ever arise, ever has arisen, ever will arise.

166
00:19:01,050 --> 00:19:13,139
[SPEAKER_01]: In this world, the ultimate nature of everything is perception and feeling, which is what determines your experience in the most general manner of your mind.

167
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:27,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why whenever they would progress through these different establishments, higher establishments of the mind, in terms of the jhānas and so on, then the infinite space, they would come down to perception and feeling, and then, oh, there is no higher than this.

168
00:19:27,910 --> 00:19:30,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, that is surmounting the phenomenological

169
00:19:32,479 --> 00:19:35,560
[SPEAKER_01]: World, basically, in every shape or form, it doesn't matter.

170
00:19:37,401 --> 00:19:40,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's in a sense of the utmost general thing.

171
00:19:40,523 --> 00:19:42,024
[SPEAKER_01]: It's to be felt and to be perceived.

172
00:19:42,664 --> 00:19:43,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, it doesn't exist.

173
00:19:44,645 --> 00:19:50,208
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when people think about things that they haven't perceived or felt, well, their thoughts are being felt and perceived.

174
00:19:50,228 --> 00:19:53,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, ultimately, if you come to the root of the experience, is the perception and the feeling.

175
00:19:54,390 --> 00:19:57,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why the Buddha said, the mind is the forerunner of all things.

176
00:19:59,566 --> 00:20:06,989
[SPEAKER_01]: It determines, basically, the direction of your thought, and whether you're going to be aware of the nature in regard to it, or just be focused on the particular.

177
00:20:09,010 --> 00:20:13,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from there, the sutta talks about abandoning the five hindrances.

178
00:20:14,472 --> 00:20:27,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are covetousness, or sensuality, ill-will, sloth and torpor, restlessness, remorse, and doubt, as they're generally translated.

179
00:20:28,397 --> 00:20:28,957
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

180
00:20:29,909 --> 00:20:32,651
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha gives various similes about that too.

181
00:20:33,292 --> 00:20:35,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Like he says, eh...

182
00:20:36,775 --> 00:20:47,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose a man were to take a loan and undertake business, and his business were to succeed, so that he could repay all the money of the old loan, and there would remain enough extra to maintain a wife.

183
00:20:48,524 --> 00:20:52,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, on considering this, he would be glad and full of joy.

184
00:20:53,088 --> 00:20:53,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Eh...

185
00:20:54,065 --> 00:21:04,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, too, when these five hindrances are unabandoned in himself, the bhikkhu sees them respectively as a debt and so on with the other similes.

186
00:21:05,815 --> 00:21:19,266
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these hindrances... Can you just kind of start by saying, just to like... What are they hindering exactly?

187
00:21:19,286 --> 00:21:20,227
[SPEAKER_00]: What makes them hindering?

188
00:21:20,247 --> 00:21:23,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they hinder the establishment of the mind.

189
00:21:24,335 --> 00:21:34,019
[SPEAKER_01]: On that general nature of things, they sort of narrow you down, they hinder you, they sort of bring you down into that more particular direction.

190
00:21:34,659 --> 00:21:40,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, more of a hindrance means more of a binding for your mind.

191
00:21:40,062 --> 00:21:43,463
[SPEAKER_01]: It is more of that kind of tunnel vision towards one particular content.

192
00:21:45,544 --> 00:21:49,886
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have weakened hindrances, in which case the mind would still be able to

193
00:21:55,697 --> 00:22:08,650
[SPEAKER_01]: The hindrances can be weakened as well, in which case the mind can still be aware of the general background and have like a degree of the pull of those hindrances in terms of degree of the clouding, so to speak.

194
00:22:09,451 --> 00:22:11,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the mind is free from hindrances...

195
00:22:13,795 --> 00:22:21,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the mind is completely free from hindrances, the mind can be established upon the general nature of things, without being absorbed and pulled by the content.

196
00:22:21,457 --> 00:22:24,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing, that's what's in common to every hindrance.

197
00:22:24,698 --> 00:22:28,239
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a certain contextual pull, so to speak.

198
00:22:28,840 --> 00:22:34,101
[SPEAKER_01]: You're pulled by a content, as in content of your doubt, sensuality, ill-will.

199
00:22:34,361 --> 00:22:37,542
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just have ill-will without any particular object of your ill-will.

200
00:22:38,602 --> 00:22:41,283
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to end up being pinned down onto something.

201
00:22:42,103 --> 00:22:47,324
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what all the hindrances have in common.

202
00:22:47,544 --> 00:22:49,885
[SPEAKER_01]: They're taking you in the direction of the content.

203
00:22:49,905 --> 00:22:51,545
[SPEAKER_01]: They're taking you in the direction of sensuality.

204
00:22:52,825 --> 00:22:58,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why they're directly against the proper samādhi, proper establishment of the mind.

205
00:23:01,667 --> 00:23:07,228
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by doing all the things previously, like the restraint and the moderation and eating and whatnot,

206
00:23:08,568 --> 00:23:11,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it almost like strengthening your mind against hindrances?

207
00:23:12,730 --> 00:23:14,310
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... yeah, it's a preparation.

208
00:23:14,470 --> 00:23:22,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like people... you won't see a hindrance if you, by... habitually, you're already well within... acting within the contextual book.

209
00:23:23,034 --> 00:23:26,355
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're pulled by the content, and you had no restraint,

210
00:23:27,092 --> 00:23:30,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You had no self-reflection, means you're not aware of the hindrance.

211
00:23:30,275 --> 00:23:32,337
[SPEAKER_01]: For you, it's just you and the content.

212
00:23:32,357 --> 00:23:40,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to stop exercising behavior that's already bound with the content of your senses, which is the sense restraint.

213
00:23:40,706 --> 00:23:46,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you do that, then, the general nature of that pull of a hindrance, that's when it becomes revealed.

214
00:23:48,779 --> 00:23:51,261
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why usually people would give in to the content.

215
00:23:51,381 --> 00:23:59,507
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because bearing that pull of that hindrance is unpleasant and they don't know any other way of overcoming it.

216
00:23:59,547 --> 00:24:03,129
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only other way is to forget about it through giving in to the content.

217
00:24:09,134 --> 00:24:16,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, having abandoned the hindrances, the Buddha talks about going into the four jhānas.

218
00:24:18,755 --> 00:24:32,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Having the three true knowledges, you know, the knowledge of past lives, the knowledge of beings being born, passing away, and knowledge of portable truths, and one becoming an arhant, the basis of that.

219
00:24:33,083 --> 00:24:43,188
[SPEAKER_00]: I was planning to ask more about, like, jhāna in general in a later video, but with regards to this gradual path,

220
00:24:45,420 --> 00:24:55,473
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't sound like it's like a linear thing, so to speak, in terms of, like, you get sīla to 95% and then you progress to restraint or something.

221
00:24:55,533 --> 00:24:57,376
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a perpendicular thing.

222
00:24:57,416 --> 00:25:01,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you can say linear, but not linear in a sense that you can observe the line.

223
00:25:02,570 --> 00:25:07,456
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's perpendicular because... well, it goes right through your being, right through your point of view.

224
00:25:07,896 --> 00:25:09,258
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what establishment of mind is.

225
00:25:09,338 --> 00:25:16,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why developing the general background that sort of transcends your limited point of view that you have cultivated through engagement with the senses.

226
00:25:17,538 --> 00:25:26,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, yeah, there is a certain gradual development, but it's not linear, exactly like, oh, now I do one, then I do two, then I do three, then I do four, five, six, seven.

227
00:25:26,842 --> 00:25:32,824
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I often say it's not a mechanical method, which is usually how many people would like to approach their meditation.

228
00:25:32,904 --> 00:25:36,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Just tell me what to do and what to look for, so I don't have to think about anything.

229
00:25:40,147 --> 00:25:40,888
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another

230
00:25:42,092 --> 00:25:48,296
[SPEAKER_00]: An elicitation of a less broad, but still, I guess, gradual path.

231
00:25:48,477 --> 00:25:56,002
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes up in a few different places, but this one in particular is from the Samana-pāla Sutta, from Gīgānikāya, number two.

232
00:25:57,303 --> 00:26:03,107
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes much the same as the previous sutta, just more expansive.

233
00:26:04,188 --> 00:26:10,733
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, after it talks about the hindrances being abandoned, it says,

234
00:26:12,982 --> 00:26:20,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing that these five hindrances have been abandoned in himself, delight arises.

235
00:26:20,446 --> 00:26:23,007
[SPEAKER_00]: When he is delighted, bliss arises.

236
00:26:23,368 --> 00:26:24,468
[SPEAKER_00]: That's Pīti.

237
00:26:25,309 --> 00:26:28,971
[SPEAKER_00]: When his mind is blissful, his body becomes tranquil.

238
00:26:29,831 --> 00:26:32,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Tranquil in body, he feels happiness.

239
00:26:33,213 --> 00:26:36,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Being happy, his mind becomes composed.

240
00:26:37,135 --> 00:26:39,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, from there, it starts talking about jhānas.

241
00:26:39,836 --> 00:26:40,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

242
00:26:43,528 --> 00:26:48,571
[SPEAKER_00]: What kind of delight and bliss and happiness are we referring to here?

243
00:26:48,651 --> 00:26:55,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it seems to me that perhaps it's not what we necessarily think of when we initially think of those terms.

244
00:26:56,175 --> 00:27:00,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's because most often those terms are inseparable from sensuality.

245
00:27:01,719 --> 00:27:02,619
[SPEAKER_01]: The sensual joy.

246
00:27:03,039 --> 00:27:08,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Joy on account of gaining, having things, seeing things, hearing things, tasting things, touching things, smelling things.

247
00:27:11,084 --> 00:27:12,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is completely the opposite direction of that.

248
00:27:13,622 --> 00:27:25,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why by recognizing the extent of sensuality, the extent of that contextual perceptual pull, and because it's a pull, means you're kind of enslaved to it.

249
00:27:26,374 --> 00:27:30,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why by recognizing it, by fully discerning it, you free yourself from it.

250
00:27:32,123 --> 00:27:39,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's why the Buddha would say that somebody who is free from those hindrances, from that kind of pull, he experienced joy.

251
00:27:39,348 --> 00:27:41,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you really feel like you're that free now.

252
00:27:41,950 --> 00:27:47,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there is no more burden pressuring you or dragging or pulling you in that sort of threatening manner.

253
00:27:48,063 --> 00:28:07,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're free from it, which then means you're free from the entire sphere, scope of things that you used to have to do on account of sensuality, just because you were a victim, you were a subject to that displeasing amount of pull in the background of your mind.

254
00:28:07,209 --> 00:28:14,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So, naturally then, being free from that kind of burden, that kind of pressure, it feels like a relief.

255
00:28:16,308 --> 00:28:19,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that relief is a... it's a joyous thing.

256
00:28:19,150 --> 00:28:23,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And that relief results in calmness, in seeing things clearly, because you're relieved now.

257
00:28:23,373 --> 00:28:24,894
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have anything to worry about.

258
00:28:28,216 --> 00:28:31,399
[SPEAKER_00]: How about when it's mentioned, like, the body being tranquil?

259
00:28:32,059 --> 00:28:36,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how do we think about that in a non-sensual way, like getting a massage or something?

260
00:28:38,484 --> 00:28:40,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, that's still sensual, isn't it?

261
00:28:40,562 --> 00:28:40,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

262
00:28:40,702 --> 00:28:41,483
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm saying.

263
00:28:41,523 --> 00:28:43,365
[SPEAKER_00]: How do we think about it in a non-sensual way?

264
00:28:43,685 --> 00:28:43,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

265
00:28:43,965 --> 00:28:50,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it means the tranquility that comes out of freedom from hindrances.

266
00:28:51,171 --> 00:28:53,674
[SPEAKER_01]: That relief is felt in the body.

267
00:28:53,694 --> 00:28:55,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, the body is tranquil.

268
00:28:56,236 --> 00:28:57,057
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is calm.

269
00:28:57,477 --> 00:28:58,458
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is pleasant.

270
00:28:59,679 --> 00:29:00,079
[SPEAKER_01]: And that...

271
00:29:00,099 --> 00:29:03,082
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm pretty much describing the pleasure that would describe the first jhāna.

272
00:29:04,052 --> 00:29:05,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, just to the right amount.

273
00:29:05,433 --> 00:29:09,656
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not spilling out of your senses, but not denying the mind.

274
00:29:09,796 --> 00:29:11,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're just left alone there.

275
00:29:11,958 --> 00:29:13,199
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha gave the simile.

276
00:29:13,259 --> 00:29:13,959
[SPEAKER_01]: What was the simile?

277
00:29:13,979 --> 00:29:18,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a... like a... like a bath powder.

278
00:29:18,983 --> 00:29:19,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's it.

279
00:29:19,604 --> 00:29:20,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Bath powder, adding water to it.

280
00:29:21,385 --> 00:29:22,586
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get the right consistency.

281
00:29:22,606 --> 00:29:24,447
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not too much water, not too little water.

282
00:29:25,608 --> 00:29:32,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can get that, not by sort of measuring the powder, but basically, by knowing what too much and too little is.

283
00:29:32,672 --> 00:29:38,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the sense for your body, you know the extent of sensual involvement.

284
00:29:39,497 --> 00:29:41,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you know the extent of non-involvement.

285
00:29:42,019 --> 00:29:43,020
[SPEAKER_01]: And to that extent...

286
00:29:43,080 --> 00:29:47,062
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to then keep denying... you don't need to go further and deny the body more than that.

287
00:29:48,263 --> 00:29:49,104
[SPEAKER_01]: You've restrained it.

288
00:29:49,484 --> 00:29:52,626
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind has been... the mind has surmounted the hindrances.

289
00:29:52,787 --> 00:30:01,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, these senses are left... there is no intent, there is no attitude, there is no action that is at the time present.

290
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:06,023
[SPEAKER_01]: But you won't get there if you've been trying to deny them, either.

291
00:30:06,063 --> 00:30:11,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't indulge them, but you can't deny them in the same manner, because

292
00:30:26,845 --> 00:30:40,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way you choose to do, you're going to end up being involved, overly involved or underly involved with them, which means it's either too much water or too little water, which means there is still pressure, either negative or positive, but the pressure is still there.

293
00:30:53,543 --> 00:31:03,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we establish that there's a, so to speak, a gradual path, gradual training to establishing the mind and composing the mind.

294
00:31:06,112 --> 00:31:09,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sometimes said that one enters

295
00:31:09,872 --> 00:31:24,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say jhāna, but really any kind of establishment of samādhi, kind of in a natural way, like you just do... keep sīla well restrained and then these things arise naturally, so to speak.

296
00:31:24,961 --> 00:31:26,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that the case here?

297
00:31:26,041 --> 00:31:29,943
[SPEAKER_00]: We just... we wait for things to arise as they do, like establishments?

298
00:31:29,983 --> 00:31:33,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if by naturally you mean automatically, no, not necessarily.

299
00:31:33,585 --> 00:31:37,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to... you need to know... well, you need to understand.

300
00:31:40,314 --> 00:31:42,034
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of establishment of mind.

301
00:31:42,074 --> 00:31:44,575
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to understand the nature of right composure.

302
00:31:46,135 --> 00:31:50,776
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't just happen to you.

303
00:31:51,556 --> 00:31:52,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't just happen to you.

304
00:31:54,496 --> 00:31:59,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if something happens to you, it's not the composure, because you haven't been composing it.

305
00:32:00,218 --> 00:32:08,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, there is a there is a whole set of prerequisites that you must develop and maintain, but then also knowing that it's about

306
00:32:09,603 --> 00:32:14,205
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the background of the particularly arisen experience, the general nature behind it.

307
00:32:14,305 --> 00:32:21,789
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you're supposed to discern it without making it a foreground, without overly attending it, without ignoring it, without under-attending it either.

308
00:32:22,629 --> 00:32:24,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something that you need to know.

309
00:32:24,650 --> 00:32:30,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you know that's how you can compose it, then eventually, through that practice, you will not necessarily

310
00:32:31,263 --> 00:32:32,524
[SPEAKER_01]: You automatically result in it.

311
00:32:33,144 --> 00:32:49,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, having... if you... if you have really perfected the sense restraint and wakefulness and mindfulness, inevitably, you will start looking in that direction, because you're already significantly withdrawn from the particular... from the pool of the particular sense content.

312
00:32:51,212 --> 00:32:52,873
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, it's not... it's not guaranteed.

313
00:32:53,293 --> 00:32:54,854
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be good if there is some instruction.

314
00:32:56,494 --> 00:32:57,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You might get a hint of it or something,

315
00:32:58,665 --> 00:33:02,146
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you would actually have to willingly turn there and go that way.

316
00:33:02,566 --> 00:33:05,647
[SPEAKER_01]: That's actually... that's even what the Buddha done.

317
00:33:06,828 --> 00:33:21,073
[SPEAKER_01]: He would... when he was trying to figure out what could be the way of developing the mind fully, properly, he remembered that kind of pleasant, peaceful, burden-free, pressure-free experience he had under the tree, when he was still young.

318
00:33:22,313 --> 00:33:23,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And he thought, well, what if I go that way?

319
00:33:24,374 --> 00:33:27,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, by going that way, we actually understood it, developed it and so on.

320
00:33:29,353 --> 00:33:40,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Another one, one of the similar lines was when the Buddha was encouraging Mahāgama Gālānā to develop jhānas, and then the Buddha left, and Mahāgama Gālānā sat under a tree, and then he said, first jhāna, first jhāna, what is first jhāna?

321
00:33:41,399 --> 00:33:53,646
[SPEAKER_01]: And then started contemplating the factors of the first jhāna, started basically reflecting upon the description of the first jhāna that the Buddha gave, and then by understanding what that was, he developed it.

322
00:33:54,771 --> 00:34:00,776
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no delay or discrepancy between understanding and establishing your mind.

323
00:34:00,796 --> 00:34:14,269
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would praise the jhānas in themselves as well, because even without a right view, you're going to develop a lot of understanding in regard to the nature and the basic characteristics of the universal aspects of your experience.

324
00:34:15,430 --> 00:34:21,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, a person without a right view develops the jhānas, they're going to need very little instruction to become an arahant.

325
00:34:22,055 --> 00:34:25,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they've already done most of the work through that correct establishment of the mind.

326
00:34:28,698 --> 00:34:44,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Another concept that comes up quite often in the traditional literature regarding concentration is the nimitta, generally translated as like sign, broadly speaking.

327
00:34:44,484 --> 00:34:46,945
[SPEAKER_00]: They discuss that in terms of generally like a

328
00:34:47,515 --> 00:35:00,350
[SPEAKER_00]: a light that one has arise when they have a kind of a stage of concentration and then, uh, you know, you manipulate it and focus on it more and things like that, uh, anyway, the

329
00:35:01,349 --> 00:35:08,634
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we've spoken about, kind of, why it doesn't really seem to make much sense in the context of composing the mind already.

330
00:35:08,654 --> 00:35:13,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, even in the suttas in general, nimitta is always... means signs and tokens of the experience.

331
00:35:13,977 --> 00:35:15,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, what's the significance of this?

332
00:35:16,219 --> 00:35:16,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And exactly.

333
00:35:16,739 --> 00:35:19,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say, oh, the significance is this light.

334
00:35:19,501 --> 00:35:20,041
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's nothing.

335
00:35:20,061 --> 00:35:21,262
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just another perception.

336
00:35:21,482 --> 00:35:22,783
[SPEAKER_01]: A more general perception.

337
00:35:22,843 --> 00:35:24,724
[SPEAKER_01]: A more ambiguous perception.

338
00:35:25,404 --> 00:35:29,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Nimitta is the signs and tokens behind the particular perceptions.

339
00:35:29,966 --> 00:35:33,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you perceive those signs and tokens, but only in that indirect manner.

340
00:35:33,329 --> 00:35:38,133
[SPEAKER_01]: If you directly look at it, it means something else is the significance of your direct look.

341
00:35:38,987 --> 00:35:49,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, we don't have to go further than that simile, that sutta that you read, where the thoroughbred horse, he doesn't get absorbed with the golden nimitta of the color of the hay.

342
00:35:50,393 --> 00:35:50,513
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

343
00:35:50,593 --> 00:35:53,975
[SPEAKER_01]: He looks at what's the significance of me being fed this hay?

344
00:35:54,695 --> 00:35:59,658
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, what are gonna... what is the horse trainer gonna ask me to do because of this?

345
00:36:00,078 --> 00:36:00,938
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the background?

346
00:36:01,378 --> 00:36:02,059
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nimitta.

347
00:36:02,739 --> 00:36:03,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So, exactly, developing

348
00:36:04,672 --> 00:36:14,835
[SPEAKER_01]: The discernment of the background means already you are not being pulled by the particular things in your foreground, so to speak.

349
00:36:15,195 --> 00:36:23,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So, developing that kind of discernment of the background means developing the nimitta in that particular regard to that experience.

350
00:36:23,578 --> 00:36:30,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as kind of a crude example, just because it's a very kind of vague term, like a sign and token of things,

351
00:36:31,140 --> 00:36:34,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say we have like a literal sign, like a stop sign.

352
00:36:34,762 --> 00:36:34,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

353
00:36:34,962 --> 00:36:38,184
[SPEAKER_00]: The nimitta is not like the actual sign.

354
00:36:38,764 --> 00:36:40,045
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the meaning of stop.

355
00:36:40,445 --> 00:36:41,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't pass this line.

356
00:36:41,706 --> 00:36:42,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

357
00:36:42,206 --> 00:36:43,447
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the nimitta.

358
00:36:43,787 --> 00:36:45,868
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people would think nimitta is the sign.

359
00:36:47,068 --> 00:36:49,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the actual physical board that says stop.

360
00:36:50,110 --> 00:36:51,551
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

361
00:36:51,611 --> 00:36:57,574
[SPEAKER_01]: There is an object that you're perceiving that has the nimitta of certain implication of don't go past this point.

362
00:36:58,630 --> 00:36:59,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the nimitta.

363
00:36:59,791 --> 00:37:01,852
[SPEAKER_01]: And nimitta is always in that background.

364
00:37:03,853 --> 00:37:15,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, particular things, in your experience, that you through... through your perception, point, obviously, at various backgrounds, and that's why the signs and the tokens of the mind, because the mind is always on that background.

365
00:37:16,882 --> 00:37:22,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the sign of the mind, cittanimitta, that's... that's like a... almost like a prerequisite for... for the right view, for saṭipatthana.

366
00:37:23,315 --> 00:37:28,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, basically, you need to know the direction of the development of mind in order to begin to develop your mind.

367
00:37:28,661 --> 00:37:33,126
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why cittanimitta is a prerequisite for samādhiti.

368
00:37:33,927 --> 00:37:37,151
[SPEAKER_00]: And here's... here's that... the sutra that kind of goes into that.

369
00:37:37,171 --> 00:37:41,396
[SPEAKER_00]: That's from the Book of Sixes of the Anguttara Nikaya, Sutta 68.

370
00:37:41,416 --> 00:37:41,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It says...

371
00:37:44,517 --> 00:38:00,800
[SPEAKER_00]: bhikkhus, for a bhikkhu who delights in company, who takes pleasure in company, who engages in delight in company, who delights in a crowd, so on and so forth, crowd, that he should enjoy being alone and secluded, is not a possibility.

372
00:38:01,460 --> 00:38:07,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Not enjoying being alone and secluded, that he should pick up the sign of the mind, is not a possibility.

373
00:38:07,901 --> 00:38:12,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Not picking up the sign of the mind, that he should fulfill right view, is not a possibility.

374
00:38:12,863 --> 00:38:17,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having fulfilled right view, that he should fulfill right composure, is not a possibility.

375
00:38:18,347 --> 00:38:22,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having fulfilled right composure, that he should abandon the fetters, is not a possibility.

376
00:38:22,950 --> 00:38:27,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having abandoned the fetters, that he should realize nibbāna, is not a possibility.

377
00:38:28,073 --> 00:38:35,238
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of self-explanatory, based on... well, based on all the previous things I've said.

378
00:38:36,893 --> 00:38:37,714
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just exactly that.

379
00:38:37,734 --> 00:38:47,461
[SPEAKER_01]: The perpendicular development, it's not one, two, three, four, five, but by being withdrawn from the people, by being restrained from the senses, you start finding peace and solitude.

380
00:38:47,481 --> 00:38:54,846
[SPEAKER_01]: By being in solitude, you start to see the background of your mind clearer, because you're not constantly bombarded by the sense pool, as in by the particular content.

381
00:38:55,927 --> 00:39:06,074
[SPEAKER_01]: By not being bombarded by the sense pool, by being properly established in that solitude, you start to discern the significance of that background, the nimitta of that background, the signs and tokens of that background, which is that mind.

382
00:39:06,931 --> 00:39:08,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you develop the understanding.

383
00:39:09,353 --> 00:39:11,034
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you begin to practice right.

384
00:39:11,775 --> 00:39:16,618
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no correct samādhi without the right view, as that sutta and many other suttas would often say.

385
00:39:17,419 --> 00:39:21,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another thing that people often choose to ignore, because they find meditation helpful.

386
00:39:22,483 --> 00:39:29,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says you should stop trying to meditate, but don't think you know how to meditate rightly, unless you have the right view.

387
00:39:33,205 --> 00:39:42,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's another sutta dealing with signs and tokens from the 47th saṃyuta, the satipaṭṭhāna saṃyuta, the 8th sutta.

388
00:39:42,848 --> 00:39:44,529
[SPEAKER_00]: It's called The Cook.

389
00:39:47,049 --> 00:39:57,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, suppose a foolish, inexperienced, unskillful cook were to present the king or royal minister with various portions of curries of various flavors.

390
00:39:58,754 --> 00:40:04,740
[SPEAKER_00]: That foolish, inexperienced, unskillful cook does not pick up the sign of his master's preference.

391
00:40:05,320 --> 00:40:15,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Today, this curry pleased my master, for he reached for this one, he took a lot of this one, he spoke in praise of this one, or this one, or that one, and so on and so forth.

392
00:40:16,152 --> 00:40:20,776
[SPEAKER_00]: That foolish, inexperienced and skillful cook does not gain clothing, wages or gifts.

393
00:40:21,276 --> 00:40:21,957
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

394
00:40:22,397 --> 00:40:27,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in this way, this foolish cook does not pick up the sign of his master's preference.

395
00:40:28,302 --> 00:40:29,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So too bhikkhus.

396
00:40:29,803 --> 00:40:41,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Some foolish, inexperienced and skillful bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body and the body, ardent, aware, mindful, and removes covetousness and displeasure in regards to the world.

397
00:40:42,197 --> 00:40:48,799
[SPEAKER_00]: While he dwells contemplating the body in the body, his mind does not become concentrated, or composed.

398
00:40:49,299 --> 00:40:50,800
[SPEAKER_00]: His defilements are not abandoned.

399
00:40:51,140 --> 00:40:53,160
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not pick up that sign.

400
00:40:53,320 --> 00:40:58,602
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on with the other three satipaṭṭhāna.

401
00:40:58,642 --> 00:41:07,384
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it seems here almost like, like, intuition when it's described in this simile.

402
00:41:08,025 --> 00:41:09,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it means basically...

403
00:41:10,040 --> 00:41:12,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing what you do, knowing what you're doing.

404
00:41:14,202 --> 00:41:23,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't know what you're doing in the beginning, but you're learning by seeing the results of it, by seeing the the implications of it, by seeing how it affects things.

405
00:41:24,186 --> 00:41:29,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, after a while, you become skilled, that cook becomes skilled, he doesn't even have to think, he knows exactly how to please the king.

406
00:41:30,416 --> 00:41:34,557
[SPEAKER_01]: It was exactly what he likes to eat on what days and after he's been eating this or that.

407
00:41:34,657 --> 00:41:34,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

408
00:41:34,837 --> 00:41:35,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's been looking at it.

409
00:41:35,958 --> 00:41:39,139
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't just looking at, oh, he ate this, he didn't eat this, that's it.

410
00:41:39,159 --> 00:41:40,679
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just make this, I won't make that.

411
00:41:41,260 --> 00:41:42,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it's this, sometimes it's that.

412
00:41:43,400 --> 00:41:50,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to see that, you must stop being concerned and absorbed only with what you see, directly with your perception.

413
00:41:50,803 --> 00:41:57,445
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to start seeing things indirectly in that background, that are there, simultaneously present with whatever else you're perceiving.

414
00:41:58,617 --> 00:42:01,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same goes actually even contemplating body in the body correctly.

415
00:42:02,280 --> 00:42:03,060
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already that.

416
00:42:03,881 --> 00:42:11,166
[SPEAKER_01]: When you step into the background in regard to your body, that's how the body is twice, eh... doubly present, so to speak.

417
00:42:11,206 --> 00:42:16,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Background in regard to a particular experience is the... is the background of that particular experience.

418
00:42:17,471 --> 00:42:18,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's inseparable from.

419
00:42:19,493 --> 00:42:25,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can be absorbed with the body, or if you reflect mindfully upon body, then you see body in that body.

420
00:42:26,103 --> 00:42:33,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you create a buffer zone by stepping back into the general, without looking at it, in the same direct particular manner.

421
00:42:33,688 --> 00:42:34,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you leave...

422
00:42:34,648 --> 00:42:41,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, there is a body here, and now there's you, mindful, off yourself, seated, paying attention to this particular body.

423
00:42:41,873 --> 00:42:43,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's body in the body.

424
00:42:45,115 --> 00:42:50,318
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, see the body further within the body, further within the body, further within the body.

425
00:42:50,358 --> 00:42:54,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's not even... You lost the sight of the initial body, let alone seeing the further bodies.

426
00:42:58,442 --> 00:43:21,490
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it's... when it's saying here that the unskilled bhikkhu, he's trying to contemplate the body and the body or feelings and feelings or whatever, yet he's still at that same time, he's not becoming composed, he doesn't pick up that sign... Well, actually, when... that unskilled bhikkhu, it's actually relatively skilled for the contemporary practices of meditation.

427
00:43:21,570 --> 00:43:23,671
[SPEAKER_01]: He's at least doing body and the body correctly.

428
00:43:23,711 --> 00:43:25,412
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha didn't dispute that.

429
00:43:26,286 --> 00:43:29,387
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, he says that on account of that practice, he doesn't look further.

430
00:43:30,007 --> 00:43:38,251
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't see the signs and the signs and the tokens of that experience of mindful body in the body, because that's how you get to understand it.

431
00:43:40,972 --> 00:43:42,632
[SPEAKER_01]: But initially, he's correct.

432
00:43:42,713 --> 00:43:47,815
[SPEAKER_01]: His practice of body in the body, ardent, resolute, withdrawn from sensuality, he's doing that very well.

433
00:43:48,855 --> 00:43:53,997
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm saying is that most of the meditation approaches people take today, they don't even do that correctly.

434
00:43:54,650 --> 00:43:58,012
[SPEAKER_01]: They focus on like imaginary body within the further imaginary.

435
00:43:58,092 --> 00:43:59,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not even a body anymore.

436
00:43:59,372 --> 00:44:06,575
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes theories and almost like abstract notions that you perceive, that you think you're perceiving.

437
00:44:07,336 --> 00:44:13,458
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to perceive them in any shape or form that you believe you can, it will require you to step outside of your experience.

438
00:44:14,179 --> 00:44:18,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you think you're perceiving, means all you're doing is engaging in a view that you are outside of your experience.

439
00:44:20,090 --> 00:44:25,912
[SPEAKER_01]: As in seeing yourself in that impersonal manner, independent of the five aggregates and of what has arisen.

440
00:44:26,493 --> 00:44:26,993
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

441
00:44:27,013 --> 00:44:29,694
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not even seeing the body, let alone seeing the body in the body.

442
00:44:31,154 --> 00:44:32,335
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you have to go further.

443
00:44:32,375 --> 00:44:40,518
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody who does learn how to do properly composure based on mindfulness of body within the body, then he needs to start looking at the background of that.

444
00:44:41,739 --> 00:44:44,860
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how he develops signs and tokens in regard to that experience.

445
00:44:46,597 --> 00:44:54,342
[SPEAKER_01]: In the background of him doing mindfulness of the body... Well, the significance, the tokens, the signs and tokens of the experience of being mindful of body in the body.

446
00:44:55,382 --> 00:44:56,603
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not something you can do.

447
00:44:57,523 --> 00:45:02,506
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something that you can discern based on the principles of discernment that you've been cultivating up to that point.

448
00:45:03,227 --> 00:45:05,748
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't, that's what Buddha says the foolish monk.

449
00:45:06,809 --> 00:45:09,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he didn't take it as far as he could have, should have.

450
00:45:12,565 --> 00:45:18,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I wanted to start explicitly discussing jhāna itself.

451
00:45:19,870 --> 00:45:26,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any particular English translation that is appropriate for jhāna, or does nothing really seem to fit?

452
00:45:28,515 --> 00:45:38,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whatever you choose to translate it with, is going to require lots of explanation, because... well, because all the views associated with it.

453
00:45:40,422 --> 00:45:41,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no translation will be perfect.

454
00:45:42,123 --> 00:45:44,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, leaving it untranslated, it's not perfect either.

455
00:45:45,164 --> 00:45:46,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it needs to be understood.

456
00:45:48,685 --> 00:45:50,386
[SPEAKER_00]: But we at least establish it's like...

457
00:45:50,626 --> 00:45:53,287
[SPEAKER_01]: I kind of end up referring to it as establishments of mind.

458
00:45:54,007 --> 00:45:55,368
[SPEAKER_01]: I know it's a bit awkward.

459
00:45:55,388 --> 00:46:01,570
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a concise terminology, but... maybe I'll think about it.

460
00:46:01,590 --> 00:46:04,111
[SPEAKER_01]: See if I can come up with one word that I'll be satisfied with.

461
00:46:05,732 --> 00:46:06,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

462
00:46:06,232 --> 00:46:07,993
[SPEAKER_00]: We established previously that it's...

463
00:46:09,287 --> 00:46:11,750
[SPEAKER_00]: It's along the lines of composing the mind.

464
00:46:11,790 --> 00:46:17,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, let's call them... the first jhāna is the first composition.

465
00:46:17,715 --> 00:46:21,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi is composure, practice of composure, jhāna is the composition.

466
00:46:22,419 --> 00:46:28,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something like that.

467
00:46:28,084 --> 00:46:31,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Can there be samādhi that isn't jhāna, though?

468
00:46:31,467 --> 00:46:32,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure, sure.

469
00:46:33,618 --> 00:46:45,504
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, samādhi, as in composure of your mind, you can compose it in regard to, well, various approaches, various ways of looking at things.

470
00:46:46,945 --> 00:46:55,169
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's really, if your mind is developed to a sufficient extent, then you can say it's the extent of the first jhāna or the second jhāna.

471
00:46:55,209 --> 00:47:01,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if your practice is not necessarily the practice of jhāna, you might be doing other types of mindfulness and meditation.

472
00:47:04,460 --> 00:47:08,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so I'll read the formula for the first jhāna.

473
00:47:10,006 --> 00:47:17,974
[SPEAKER_01]: First jhāna is basically a way of attending to certain aspects of your experience with a developed mind.

474
00:47:19,255 --> 00:47:24,060
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you establish, that's how you compose that first jhāna, that is outside of the central domain.

475
00:47:25,361 --> 00:47:25,802
[SPEAKER_00]: We're gone.

476
00:47:27,780 --> 00:47:29,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Enters and dwells.

477
00:47:29,242 --> 00:47:30,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Means he spends time in it.

478
00:47:53,086 --> 00:47:59,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we can go into discussing, perhaps, each of these pairs of factors.

479
00:48:01,252 --> 00:48:06,676
[SPEAKER_00]: The first is secluded from sensual pleasures.

480
00:48:07,537 --> 00:48:09,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seclusion.

481
00:48:09,879 --> 00:48:17,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people say that in jhāna, you don't experience the senses at all, and that's why you're secluded.

482
00:48:17,845 --> 00:48:18,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

483
00:48:19,606 --> 00:48:22,048
[SPEAKER_01]: You've surmounted the senses, which means they're very much

484
00:48:23,648 --> 00:48:26,410
[SPEAKER_01]: The full extent of the sense domain is right in front of you.

485
00:48:27,250 --> 00:48:33,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why people can never be aware of the full extent is because they give in to the pull of the sense object way too soon.

486
00:48:34,074 --> 00:48:40,498
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't give in, then the positive object and the negative sense, the whole domain becomes, well, discerned.

487
00:48:42,059 --> 00:48:48,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the first jhāna, in another sutra the Buddha said, the first jhāna is assailed by the perceptions of sensuality.

488
00:48:49,352 --> 00:48:51,533
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what's trying to undermine your first jhāna.

489
00:48:52,133 --> 00:49:00,458
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically stepping back and seeing the whole picture, the whole nature of sensuality, senses and sense objects.

490
00:49:02,199 --> 00:49:13,965
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those senses still remain subjected to the pull of the objects, but you have surmounted them, which means you don't share that nature anymore.

491
00:49:13,985 --> 00:49:14,925
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't partake in it.

492
00:49:15,525 --> 00:49:16,686
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not being pulled.

493
00:49:17,247 --> 00:49:18,067
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses remain.

494
00:49:18,107 --> 00:49:22,992
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses and sense objects are natural, indispensable counterparts.

495
00:49:23,352 --> 00:49:24,313
[SPEAKER_01]: They define each other.

496
00:49:24,353 --> 00:49:25,154
[SPEAKER_01]: They're inseparable.

497
00:49:26,956 --> 00:49:28,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't shut them down.

498
00:49:28,718 --> 00:49:33,682
[SPEAKER_01]: You find a way out without interfering with them, without trying to deny them.

499
00:49:33,702 --> 00:49:34,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you leave them alone.

500
00:49:35,044 --> 00:49:39,648
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why what I said just a few minutes before, a few minutes ago, is that

501
00:49:40,537 --> 00:49:49,162
[SPEAKER_01]: You free yourself from that sensuality, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, by surmounting the domain of the body being there, this body present right here.

502
00:49:49,843 --> 00:49:51,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where your senses are.

503
00:49:51,503 --> 00:49:52,784
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is the five senses.

504
00:49:55,566 --> 00:50:05,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you establish, like, a mindfulness of the body there, you, you, you've established, like, the condition for the senses, so to speak.

505
00:50:06,656 --> 00:50:08,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's the right direction to go.

506
00:50:08,537 --> 00:50:18,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will know that you are... that you are outside of the central pole, when you have experienced the relief from the central pole, which is undeniable.

507
00:50:18,923 --> 00:50:25,687
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to experience relief from the central pole, means you need to understand and fully see the extent of the central pole.

508
00:50:26,788 --> 00:50:30,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just replace it with something else you think it's more lofty to look at.

509
00:50:32,891 --> 00:50:35,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, paired with that is excluded from the wholesome states.

510
00:50:37,028 --> 00:50:45,255
[SPEAKER_01]: There are also states of craving that always result then in craving for or against lust, ill-will, delusion, distraction.

511
00:50:45,355 --> 00:50:53,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Distraction means dependence on the variety of the content, boredom, all that, rooted in the same domain.

512
00:50:56,038 --> 00:51:05,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we're saying, then, that there's also no craving in regards to, like, any kinds of, like, putting crudely, like, mental phenomena, so to speak?

513
00:51:05,646 --> 00:51:05,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

514
00:51:05,847 --> 00:51:10,130
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no... You're not trying to get rid of it either, which is unwholesome.

515
00:51:19,418 --> 00:51:20,980
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not trying to get rid of it either.

516
00:51:22,098 --> 00:51:25,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're like, oh, sensuality is bad, I must get rid of it.

517
00:51:25,379 --> 00:51:29,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is bad, I must surmount it and learn how to stop interfering with it.

518
00:51:29,541 --> 00:51:33,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you interfere with it either by giving in or trying to get rid of it.

519
00:51:33,363 --> 00:51:37,704
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's exactly why the first jhāna is secluded from sensual pleasure from unwholesome states.

520
00:51:38,044 --> 00:51:41,126
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just secluded from it, but you're not trying to get rid of it either.

521
00:51:41,666 --> 00:51:44,847
[SPEAKER_01]: You leave it alone, because it's not your problem anymore.

522
00:51:44,907 --> 00:51:49,809
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your debt, it's not your pressure, it's not your, your duty and your burden.

523
00:51:49,849 --> 00:51:50,630
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, oh, I

524
00:51:56,113 --> 00:52:05,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it has the words vittaka and vichara here translated as just thinking and pondering.

525
00:52:05,735 --> 00:52:14,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Traditionally, vittaka is like... Visuddhimagga mentions like the initial strike of the mind as object.

526
00:52:14,937 --> 00:52:15,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

527
00:52:15,597 --> 00:52:17,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I don't want to go into commentary or definition.

528
00:52:17,998 --> 00:52:18,418
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

529
00:52:18,778 --> 00:52:24,819
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, once you have surmounted, once you have fully discerned,

530
00:52:25,250 --> 00:52:36,577
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of the body, the extent of sensuality and its corresponding pull, eh... once you disengage from it, so to speak, what's left there is thinking and pondering.

531
00:52:37,597 --> 00:52:41,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, it's the same thinking and pondering, minus sensuality.

532
00:52:42,420 --> 00:52:44,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're still engaged with it, so to speak.

533
00:52:44,321 --> 00:52:44,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

534
00:52:45,042 --> 00:52:50,605
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, there is nothing to fear, there's nothing to try to get rid of, because thinking and pondering was never the problem.

535
00:52:51,025 --> 00:52:52,246
[SPEAKER_01]: It was thinking and pondering

536
00:52:53,093 --> 00:52:58,894
[SPEAKER_01]: About the senses, about the sense objects, about the sense avoidance, about the sense lust and so on.

537
00:52:58,994 --> 00:52:59,834
[SPEAKER_01]: That was the problem.

538
00:53:00,455 --> 00:53:02,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was lust, aversion, delusion, they were the problem.

539
00:53:02,695 --> 00:53:03,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Always were.

540
00:53:04,735 --> 00:53:09,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Once, once you live withdrawn from it, then thinking and pondering can stay.

541
00:53:09,136 --> 00:53:11,417
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what, what defines the first jhāna.

542
00:53:12,637 --> 00:53:21,319
[SPEAKER_01]: You've surmounted the body and now what's right in front of you, usually what's right in front of people is this, is this body with the sense options and sense pulls and sense, sense engagement.

543
00:53:22,318 --> 00:53:34,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But through the discernment of that background, of that simultaneously present background in regard to your particular sense objects, you have developed discernment of phenomena of a whole domain of the body.

544
00:53:35,109 --> 00:53:37,672
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, what's in front of you is the thinking and pondering.

545
00:53:39,113 --> 00:53:42,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Not anymore the counterpart of your senses, which is sense objects.

546
00:53:44,077 --> 00:53:44,698
[SPEAKER_01]: If you sort of

547
00:53:46,426 --> 00:53:47,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Turn... well, not turn away.

548
00:53:48,527 --> 00:53:54,888
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop being mindful of thinking and pondering correctly, you are back onto the lower level, which is the level of sensuality.

549
00:53:54,928 --> 00:54:04,511
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why somebody without the right view, somebody who hasn't understood the nature of assumption, the nature of upādāna, even if they have jhānas, after the jhāna, they will go back to the sensuality.

550
00:54:04,591 --> 00:54:06,091
[SPEAKER_01]: They could go back to sensuality.

551
00:54:06,891 --> 00:54:13,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But if once you are withdrawn, paired up with the right view and right understanding, you won't be going back to it, even if you're not in the jhāna anymore.

552
00:54:18,131 --> 00:54:23,512
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, rapture and pleasure born of that seclusion.

553
00:54:23,532 --> 00:54:23,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

554
00:54:24,393 --> 00:54:27,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think that you mentioned before, like, the joy of freedom.

555
00:54:27,813 --> 00:54:28,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the relief.

556
00:54:28,954 --> 00:54:33,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine you have a pressure, like a debt, to pay back.

557
00:54:33,695 --> 00:54:35,715
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, the debt collectors are there out for you.

558
00:54:35,735 --> 00:54:40,976
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know when they're going to pounce and come and impound your house and take away and whatever, imprison you.

559
00:54:41,456 --> 00:54:42,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a big burden.

560
00:54:42,477 --> 00:54:43,677
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a big stress to carry.

561
00:54:44,483 --> 00:54:46,404
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of a sensual world.

562
00:54:46,604 --> 00:54:49,866
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always something pressuring you, pulling you, going against you.

563
00:54:50,786 --> 00:54:57,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stepping outside of the entire possibility of the sensual world and sensual pulls, it's a tremendous relief.

564
00:54:58,330 --> 00:55:03,493
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why first jhāna is modern enough to become an arahant, an anāgāmī.

565
00:55:04,454 --> 00:55:11,738
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha mentions in some suttas that unlike pleasure of the senses, there's nothing blameworthy.

566
00:55:11,978 --> 00:55:12,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

567
00:55:12,618 --> 00:55:14,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you can't crave.

568
00:55:14,912 --> 00:55:18,194
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't crave for more of a relief.

569
00:55:19,294 --> 00:55:22,996
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either relieved of that which was a problem, or you're not.

570
00:55:24,837 --> 00:55:28,119
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're relieved from a problem, it means there's no more problem whatsoever.

571
00:55:28,760 --> 00:55:29,640
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a full relief.

572
00:55:30,480 --> 00:55:31,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't crave for more of it.

573
00:55:31,921 --> 00:55:36,024
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inconceivable.

574
00:55:36,044 --> 00:55:40,526
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can't crave for more of the pleasure of the relief, the pleasure of the jhāna.

575
00:55:41,665 --> 00:55:47,669
[SPEAKER_01]: If people crave for it, if people think, oh, you can't have enough of it, or you need more of it, that's just another sensuality.

576
00:55:48,350 --> 00:55:51,071
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of sensuality.

577
00:55:51,151 --> 00:55:52,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting more, needing more.

578
00:55:55,014 --> 00:55:57,396
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that context, it may be getting a little ahead.

579
00:55:58,076 --> 00:56:04,420
[SPEAKER_00]: But, like, in terms of, like, the further jhānas, then, obviously, they become more refined, more things...

580
00:56:04,781 --> 00:56:06,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the refinement of the same principle.

581
00:56:07,632 --> 00:56:17,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, you have abandoned the counterpart of your sense objects, of your sense bases, of your body, which is basically the sense object, is the counterpart.

582
00:56:18,214 --> 00:56:21,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's in front of you is the thinking and pondering.

583
00:56:21,435 --> 00:56:24,356
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you can go further and abandon thinking and pondering.

584
00:56:25,736 --> 00:56:29,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what's in front of you is the clarity of your mindfulness, and so on.

585
00:56:29,997 --> 00:56:34,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why often it would carry on into those other kind of immaterial establishments.

586
00:56:34,848 --> 00:56:38,251
[SPEAKER_01]: By following the same principles, the four jhānas, then, oh, well, is there more?

587
00:56:38,591 --> 00:56:42,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the phenomenon of space, the phenomenon of consciousness, the infinity of space, and so on.

588
00:56:43,434 --> 00:56:46,997
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, the cessation of perception of feeling, and there is no more than that.

589
00:56:47,638 --> 00:56:52,761
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, whether it's the cessation of perception of feeling, or it's the first jhāna, the principle of the establishment is the same.

590
00:56:53,182 --> 00:56:56,424
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the first jhāna is sufficient for Arahantship.

591
00:56:58,606 --> 00:57:04,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's upon the understanding of the principle, that you develop the liberation and wisdom.

592
00:57:08,932 --> 00:57:13,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Accompanying this sometimes simile is a given...

593
00:57:13,814 --> 00:57:19,536
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll give the... read out the one that the first jhāna has.

594
00:57:20,557 --> 00:57:24,038
[SPEAKER_00]: This is from Dīgha Nikāya number two.

595
00:57:24,658 --> 00:57:29,320
[SPEAKER_00]: It starts with the reading... it starts with the first jhāna formula and then it says

596
00:57:30,036 --> 00:57:34,138
[SPEAKER_00]: He fills this very body with rapture and happiness-born seclusion.

597
00:57:34,739 --> 00:57:37,540
[SPEAKER_00]: He suffuses this very body with it.

598
00:57:37,660 --> 00:57:39,741
[SPEAKER_00]: He makes the very body full of it.

599
00:57:40,062 --> 00:57:47,706
[SPEAKER_00]: He pervades the very body with it, so that there is nothing in the entire body which is not pervaded by this rapture and happiness-born seclusion.

600
00:57:48,430 --> 00:57:56,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it gives a simile of, like, sprinkled some bath powder, kneading it with moisture and stuff.

601
00:57:56,936 --> 00:58:01,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it seems to me, this could be very easily misconceived as, like, an energy flow exercise.

602
00:58:01,720 --> 00:58:02,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

603
00:58:02,741 --> 00:58:05,783
[SPEAKER_00]: How should we understand it properly?

604
00:58:06,003 --> 00:58:09,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you have to come back to that notion of relief.

605
00:58:10,747 --> 00:58:16,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine you've been harassed by something, say, a wild animal that you've been chained to.

606
00:58:17,108 --> 00:58:19,710
[SPEAKER_01]: When the animal ran, you were just dragged with it.

607
00:58:20,850 --> 00:58:27,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you were either basically at the mercy of an animal, or you had to fight it back, and constantly drag it, and sort of dig your heels in, and endure it.

608
00:58:29,396 --> 00:58:39,482
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't know that the problem is not the animal, but you being bound up with it, because of that, subjected to the things that animal wants,

609
00:58:41,040 --> 00:58:43,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of not knowing that, you don't know how to free yourself from it.

610
00:58:44,602 --> 00:58:51,507
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, people might be blaming what animal wants to get, what animal wants to eat, where it drags you, might be blaming the animal.

611
00:58:52,167 --> 00:58:58,051
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't know how to leave the animal alone, nor do they know how to step outside from the the animal range.

612
00:58:59,292 --> 00:59:04,575
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do, then you can literally have it sitting right in front of you, but you know you won't go there with your hand.

613
00:59:05,656 --> 00:59:07,337
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't get hooked again onto it.

614
00:59:08,037 --> 00:59:09,979
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're not hooked, it won't do anything to you.

615
00:59:11,117 --> 00:59:22,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's mindfulness of that relief in regard to the extent for which you neither indulge, nor deny, nor try to get rid of.

616
00:59:23,241 --> 00:59:26,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing the right amount, discerning it.

617
00:59:26,582 --> 00:59:33,165
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another sutra where the Buddha said the monk doesn't attain the jhāna because he does not attend to the theme of the jhāna.

618
00:59:33,685 --> 00:59:39,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, he does not attend to the theme of the extent that he needs to develop.

619
00:59:41,107 --> 00:59:48,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Of extent of not completely ignoring, but not completely indulging either, not trying to get rid of, but not pretending it's not there as well.

620
00:59:50,012 --> 00:59:54,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, in order to attend to the theme of something, you need to know what that theme is.

621
00:59:55,135 --> 00:59:56,656
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have a hint.

622
00:59:56,696 --> 00:59:59,138
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have signs and tokens of that theme.

623
00:59:59,658 --> 01:00:03,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And the simile the Buddha gave is that cow, the mountain cow that

624
01:00:03,733 --> 01:00:13,618
[SPEAKER_01]: wants to go to the pastures that hasn't been before, and then steps on one rock, but without sort of steadying itself in it regularly, he wants to step on the other one, and then falls in between the two.

625
01:00:14,579 --> 01:00:17,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, jhāna is not like a culmination of some practice.

626
01:00:18,301 --> 01:00:19,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Jhāna is the right practice.

627
01:00:19,982 --> 01:00:20,722
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the culmination.

628
01:00:20,742 --> 01:00:22,203
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no culmination to it.

629
01:00:23,264 --> 01:00:29,407
[SPEAKER_01]: The joy of jhāna is the joy of doing it, so to speak, in that correct manner, which is a manner of non-doing, pretty much.

630
01:00:30,227 --> 01:00:31,448
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can't pray for it.

631
01:00:32,212 --> 01:00:34,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You can crave only for something that's an object.

632
01:00:34,073 --> 01:00:36,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't crave for something that has no goal in it.

633
01:00:38,455 --> 01:00:43,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the goal of jhāna is the doing the theme of jhāna, not the result of jhāna.

634
01:00:43,818 --> 01:00:46,499
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasure of jhāna is the pleasure of doing the jhāna.

635
01:00:47,280 --> 01:00:52,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he pervades it like this, he pervades it like that, he thinks, he ponders, he withdraws, he studies it.

636
01:00:53,083 --> 01:00:55,244
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the pleasure comes from.

637
01:00:55,404 --> 01:00:59,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, people think, I'll do this, I'll meditate, I'll concentrate, I'll concentrate, and then I have the experience, and

638
01:01:01,322 --> 01:01:05,823
[SPEAKER_01]: The culmination of joy and whatever light, that's my jhāna.

639
01:01:05,863 --> 01:01:06,003
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

640
01:01:06,143 --> 01:01:12,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Jhāna is that doing which means you can be doing the jhāna without still getting the pleasure of jhāna.

641
01:01:13,705 --> 01:01:16,446
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why your mind hasn't been steadied in the jhāna.

642
01:01:16,566 --> 01:01:18,247
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you're not doing the jhāna.

643
01:01:18,947 --> 01:01:26,149
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, again, as I said before, you can do other practices that would equal to the strength of the mind of the jhāna, but not steady your mind in jhāna.

644
01:01:28,329 --> 01:01:30,170
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasure of jhāna is the pleasure of doing it.

645
01:01:31,227 --> 01:01:48,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means people should stop expecting the great kind of tremendous fountain of joy pouring out as an ecstatic result of their concentration practice, but instead focus on discerning and fully understanding what the theme of jhāna is, which is a theme of surmounting the central domain, and then do it.

646
01:01:49,253 --> 01:01:51,814
[SPEAKER_01]: And through doing it, it's going to become pleasant doing it.

647
01:01:52,454 --> 01:02:02,178
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you become established upon that pleasure and experience that relief that, oh, while I'm engaged like this, while I'm established like this, no amount of unwholesome can enter me.

648
01:02:02,358 --> 01:02:03,778
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's just a tremendous relief.

649
01:02:04,759 --> 01:02:07,220
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what pleasure of jhāna is.

650
01:02:07,260 --> 01:02:08,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of doing it rightly.

651
01:02:11,581 --> 01:02:12,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, is there further?

652
01:02:12,862 --> 01:02:13,242
[SPEAKER_01]: There is.

653
01:02:13,262 --> 01:02:14,122
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a second jhāna.

654
01:02:14,142 --> 01:02:14,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there further?

655
01:02:14,722 --> 01:02:15,143
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a third.

656
01:02:15,463 --> 01:02:16,343
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a fourth jhāna.

657
01:02:17,583 --> 01:02:23,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very, sort of, it can be a big obstacle for people, the ingrained assumption.

658
01:02:23,446 --> 01:02:26,227
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm speaking from my own experience.

659
01:02:26,387 --> 01:02:34,230
[SPEAKER_01]: The expecting, like jhānas or whatever, samādhi, to result in this experience, that kind of overwhelms you.

660
01:02:34,370 --> 01:02:36,211
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, it doesn't result in it.

661
01:02:37,311 --> 01:02:38,092
[SPEAKER_01]: You build it.

662
01:02:38,872 --> 01:02:44,314
[SPEAKER_01]: And through building it, that's where your pleasure gets experienced.

663
01:02:44,374 --> 01:02:45,655
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you can't crave for it.

664
01:02:47,948 --> 01:02:49,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not something that's given that you want.

665
01:02:50,429 --> 01:02:51,690
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either you do it or you don't do it.

666
01:02:52,030 --> 01:02:54,071
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't do it, you can't have it.

667
01:02:54,111 --> 01:02:57,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... it's inconceivable to even crave it.

668
01:03:00,294 --> 01:03:03,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, studying the mind in the theme of jhāna is the practice of jhāna.

669
01:03:04,176 --> 01:03:08,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if there is no pleasure in regard to it yet, there is a practice of jhāna.

670
01:03:08,458 --> 01:03:09,538
[SPEAKER_01]: He's cultivating jhāna.

671
01:03:10,099 --> 01:03:11,699
[SPEAKER_01]: He's studying his mind in the jhāna.

672
01:03:11,719 --> 01:03:13,860
[SPEAKER_01]: He's attending to the theme of jhāna rightly.

673
01:03:14,901 --> 01:03:17,162
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's developed to a sufficient degree,

674
01:03:18,057 --> 01:03:23,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Through sufficient repetition and diligence, again, that's why we always praise diligence as well, because it's not given.

675
01:03:24,604 --> 01:03:34,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will recognize that that experience in itself is the experience of that joy and freedom from that, freedom from pressure, freedom from pull, that you haven't been recognizing.

676
01:03:34,515 --> 01:03:34,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

677
01:03:34,755 --> 01:03:38,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't fully discerned the theme of the jhāna, the extent of it.

678
01:03:40,315 --> 01:03:41,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the whole point.

679
01:03:41,516 --> 01:03:48,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You live next to the wild beast that drags you left and right, but you don't recognize when that beast is left alone.

680
01:03:48,940 --> 01:03:51,761
[SPEAKER_01]: By not recognizing, you don't know how to cultivate it.

681
01:03:51,781 --> 01:03:54,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you keep interfering with it, and you keep paying the price.

682
01:03:58,282 --> 01:04:04,005
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if someone has the idea like, I want jhāna, they don't understand jhāna...

683
01:04:04,065 --> 01:04:04,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

684
01:04:04,926 --> 01:04:12,510
[SPEAKER_01]: I want jhāna, they should divorce that idea from the almost natural expectation, oh, I want that great experience of pleasure.

685
01:04:13,091 --> 01:04:17,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's just another craving for pleasure, which is another craving for sensuality.

686
01:04:17,934 --> 01:04:20,995
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, as the Buddha said, most people end up meditating with sensuality.

687
01:04:22,456 --> 01:04:24,998
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody wants the jhāna, and you should want jhāna,

688
01:04:25,931 --> 01:04:35,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, somebody must make all the effort to understand what that jhāna is, phenomenologically, mindfully, to be seen for what it is, the extent.

689
01:04:35,461 --> 01:04:43,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Because again, the Buddha didn't say, oh, the monk doesn't know what jhāna is and he falls between these two rocks, same like the cow between those rocks, sorry.

690
01:04:43,951 --> 01:04:52,894
[SPEAKER_01]: He said the monk doesn't attend to the theme of the jhāna, because not attending this particular extent, so not too much, not too little, this is the theme.

691
01:04:53,514 --> 01:04:57,896
[SPEAKER_01]: By not attending it, he doesn't establish it.

692
01:04:57,916 --> 01:04:58,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means he has it.

693
01:04:58,996 --> 01:04:59,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody has it.

694
01:05:01,317 --> 01:05:03,078
[SPEAKER_01]: But nobody knows what it is.

695
01:05:03,118 --> 01:05:04,578
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they can't attend to it rightly.

696
01:05:05,399 --> 01:05:08,960
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they expect it wrongly, so to speak.

697
01:05:11,668 --> 01:05:25,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so it's basically that you're saying, if, please correct me if I'm wrong, that it's like, it's the pleasure of understanding a jhāna is the pleasure of the jhāna.

698
01:05:25,594 --> 01:05:26,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

699
01:05:29,116 --> 01:05:33,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the mind has recognized itself being established in that jhāna,

700
01:05:34,427 --> 01:05:36,188
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already that pleasure of that jhāna.

701
01:05:36,348 --> 01:05:37,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that makes sense.

702
01:05:37,328 --> 01:05:39,909
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like if you don't know you're relieved, then you're not relieved.

703
01:05:40,529 --> 01:05:43,250
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you cultivate the pleasure of jhāna.

704
01:05:44,871 --> 01:05:50,013
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not given to you through some practice of some technique or something, and then the pleasure of jhāna comes over you.

705
01:05:50,553 --> 01:05:53,914
[SPEAKER_01]: If pleasure is like that, that's the pleasure you can crave for.

706
01:05:53,974 --> 01:05:56,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you can crave for it, it means it's not the pleasure of the jhāna.

707
01:05:57,315 --> 01:06:01,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as the Buddha said, those... that pleasure is wholesome because you can't crave for it.

